 "approvalMessage": {
  "approvalNumber": 96,
  "billId": {
   "basePrintNo": "A4328",
   "basePrintNoStr": "A4328-2021",
   "printNo": "A4328A",
   "session": 2021,
   "version": "A"
  },
  "chapter": 728,
  "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 96 Chapter 728\n \n      MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 4328-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law and the economic\n        development  law, in relation to establishing a state geological\n        trail\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  This bill would require the Department of  Environmental  Conservation\n(DEC)  to study and designate sixty-six areas and territories around the\nState as geoparks and establish a State geological trail to promote  the\neconomic,  cultural and scientific significance of these areas. The bill\nwould direct DEC to develop a management plan for every geopark and work\nwith a State Geological Planning and  Management  Board  that  would  be\nestablished  by  the  bill to create criteria for signage, publications,\ninterpretive programs and a website.\n \n  The evaluation of sixty-six sites, each of which may be  comprised  of\nhundreds  of  acres,  and the preparation of individual management plans\nwould take several years to complete at a significant fiscal  impact  on\nDEC  alone.  Sixteen  of the sites listed in the bill are existing parks\nowned or managed by the Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preser-\nvation (OPRHP) and are therefore  already  protected,  interpreted,  and\nprogrammed  by OPRHP. It would be costly and duplicative to create a new\nBoard to develop and recommend new signage and interpretative guidelines\nfor parklands that are already subject  to  signage  and  interpretation\nguidelines implemented by OPRHP.\n \n  In addition, many of the sites listed on the bill are privately owned,\nwhich  calls  into  question  whether  DEC  would  have  access to those\nlocations for a study or the legal authority to impose a management plan\non existing land uses. While the State's geological resources are worthy\nof being showcased, the  State  must  curate  appropriate  sites  to  be\nincluded  in  the  trail  and  the process to designate sites should not\noutpace appropriations made for this  purpose.  The  Executive  and  the\nLegislature  have agreed on a number of changes to the current bill that\nwill provide DEC with discretion to designate an area as a  geopark  and\ndetermine  whether  development  of  a  management plan is prudent for a\ngiven location. The Executive looks forward to future conversations with\nthe Legislature to determine how this bill would be funded.\n \n  On the basis of this agreement, I am pleased to sign this bill.\n \n  This bill is approved.                         (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2021
